WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.551
Module 7: Culture Dig.

00:00:01.551 --> 00:00:04.582
It's a licensed
culture-assessment program,

00:00:04.582 --> 00:00:10.081
one of the Mosaic tools: an eight to
twelve week study built on Edgar Schein's

00:00:10.081 --> 00:00:12.126
three-layer model of culture.

00:00:12.126 --> 00:00:16.144
Seven small research teams
dig deep into one aspect each,

00:00:16.144 --> 00:00:19.387
while everyone else
contributes a short story.

00:00:19.387 --> 00:00:24.957
By the end of this module, which takes
about two hours, you'll be able to plan,

00:00:24.957 --> 00:00:29.257
run and report a Culture Dig,
and explain its score honestly.

00:00:29.767 --> 00:00:35.395
A Culture Mosaic shows you the shape of a
culture in about ten minutes per person.

00:00:35.395 --> 00:00:40.130
A Culture Dig asks a different
question: why is the culture this way,

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:42.052
and what is it really doing?

00:00:42.052 --> 00:00:43.631
It works in two passes.

00:00:43.631 --> 00:00:47.612
First, seven small teams,
usually two to four people each,

00:00:47.612 --> 00:00:50.220
take one aspect of the culture apiece.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:53.926
They spend two to three weeks
interviewing colleagues,

00:00:53.926 --> 00:00:57.907
collecting documents and
observing how things really work.

00:00:57.907 --> 00:01:03.535
Then everyone else in the organization is
invited to share one short story about a

00:01:03.535 --> 00:01:08.339
real experience at work, and to answer
a few quick questions about it.

00:01:08.339 --> 00:01:12.114
What the teams find shapes
what everyone else is asked.

00:01:12.114 --> 00:01:16.369
At the end, both streams come
together in one findings report.

00:01:16.369 --> 00:01:22.066
The teams explain why something happens,
and the stories show how widely it's felt.

00:01:22.066 --> 00:01:26.321
One more thing: when a client
buys Culture Dig on the website,

00:01:26.321 --> 00:01:30.783
they're buying the software, and
they run the program themselves.

00:01:30.783 --> 00:01:33.871
Mosaic facilitation is
a separate engagement.

00:01:34.367 --> 00:01:37.606
Here's the stance you
take as a practitioner,

00:01:37.606 --> 00:01:43.077
in the words Culture Dig's own analysis
follows: culture is not good or bad.

00:01:43.077 --> 00:01:47.612
Describe what it does and what
it costs, not whether it passes.

00:01:47.612 --> 00:01:53.011
A culture that punishes mistakes may
also produce very reliable compliance.

00:01:53.011 --> 00:01:56.970
A culture of heroics may ship
fast and burn people out.

00:01:56.970 --> 00:02:02.225
Your job is to show the client what
their culture does, and at what cost,

00:02:02.225 --> 00:02:05.896
so they can decide what to
keep and what to change.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.257
Think of culture as layers.

00:02:08.257 --> 00:02:13.416
On top are the artifacts, the things
a newcomer notices on day one: spaces,

00:02:13.416 --> 00:02:15.823
symbols, rituals, how meetings run.

00:02:15.823 --> 00:02:19.194
Beneath them are the
espoused beliefs and values,

00:02:19.194 --> 00:02:21.945
what the organization says about itself.

00:02:21.945 --> 00:02:27.585
And underneath it all are the basic
assumptions: beliefs so taken for granted that

00:02:27.585 --> 00:02:29.374
nobody says them out loud.

00:02:29.374 --> 00:02:32.194
That deepest layer drives behaviour most.

00:02:32.194 --> 00:02:36.527
Culture Dig names its Artifacts
domain as Schein's first layer,

00:02:36.527 --> 00:02:40.448
and its Espoused Beliefs and
Values domain as the second.

00:02:40.448 --> 00:02:44.987
No single domain is labelled
"assumptions", and that's deliberate.

00:02:44.987 --> 00:02:47.876
Assumptions can't be asked about directly.

00:02:47.876 --> 00:02:53.104
You find them in the patterns: in the
history that taught people what to do,

00:02:53.104 --> 00:02:58.606
in the stories they tell, and especially
in the gap between what's said and what

00:02:58.606 --> 00:02:59.157
happens.

00:02:59.157 --> 00:03:03.421
That last mapping is our
interpretation, to help you teach it,

00:03:03.421 --> 00:03:05.829
rather than a rule in the software.

00:03:06.333 --> 00:03:10.289
Every program has exactly
one research team per domain,

00:03:10.289 --> 00:03:13.597
and all seven teams are
created automatically.

00:03:13.597 --> 00:03:19.494
Espoused Beliefs and Values compares what
the organization says it values with the

00:03:19.494 --> 00:03:24.600
values people describe when they
explain how decisions really get made.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:30.426
HR Management Systems and Processes traces
hiring, promotion, evaluation and pay,

00:03:30.426 --> 00:03:34.525
and asks what behaviour each
system actually selects for.

00:03:34.525 --> 00:03:38.912
Organizational Artifacts documents
what a newcomer would see,

00:03:38.912 --> 00:03:41.789
and asks insiders what each thing means.

00:03:41.789 --> 00:03:47.470
Organizational History reconstructs the
events that taught the organization its

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:52.432
current assumptions: the founding,
crises, wins, mergers and layoffs.

00:03:52.432 --> 00:03:57.251
Organizational Stories collects
the anecdotes people actually tell,

00:03:57.251 --> 00:04:01.638
and asks what behaviour each
one celebrates or warns against.

00:04:01.638 --> 00:04:06.312
Rituals and Rites of Passage
observes ceremonies and transitions,

00:04:06.312 --> 00:04:10.268
how someone is welcomed in,
promoted up, or walked out.

00:04:10.268 --> 00:04:16.021
And Stakeholder Relations looks at the
boundary: how people talk about customers

00:04:16.021 --> 00:04:22.062
when they aren't in the room, and who the
organization considers "us" versus "them".

00:04:22.567 --> 00:04:25.209
A dig gathers evidence in two streams.

00:04:25.209 --> 00:04:30.216
The domain teams are a few people
looking closely: two to three weeks of

00:04:30.216 --> 00:04:35.292
interviews, documents and observation,
which bring depth and explanation.

00:04:35.292 --> 00:04:40.577
Story collection is everyone contributing
briefly: one short story and a few

00:04:40.577 --> 00:04:45.027
questions, about three minutes,
which brings breadth and weight.

00:04:45.027 --> 00:04:47.391
Neither half is enough on its own.

00:04:47.391 --> 00:04:50.312
And where they disagree, that's a finding.

00:04:50.312 --> 00:04:53.997
If a team reports that people
feel trusted to decide,

00:04:53.997 --> 00:04:58.865
but the stories in that domain read
as unpredictable and blame-driven,

00:04:58.865 --> 00:05:00.742
don't average the two away.

00:05:00.742 --> 00:05:01.855
Explain the gap.

00:05:02.367 --> 00:05:07.777
Programs can only be created by an
organization with an active license that has

00:05:07.777 --> 00:05:09.490
room for another program.

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:11.065
There are four ways in.

00:05:11.065 --> 00:05:15.243
The Mosaic team can run the
engagement and issue the license.

00:05:15.243 --> 00:05:19.010
Anyone can buy on the website,
which buys the software,

00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:22.366
not facilitation: one
program and its first wave,

00:05:22.366 --> 00:05:26.270
with later waves bought one
at a time, up to four in all.

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:31.681
Organizations with a live SurveyCraft
platform license can buy it as an add-on.

00:05:31.681 --> 00:05:35.379
And coaches can launch it
from Mosaic Coaching Studio.

00:05:35.379 --> 00:05:39.763
Remember: a website purchase
commits no Mosaic facilitator time,

00:05:39.763 --> 00:05:41.475
so never imply otherwise.

00:05:41.475 --> 00:05:46.749
And if a license lapses, programs become
read-only and the story link closes.

00:05:47.233 --> 00:05:49.402
A program runs in five phases.

00:05:49.402 --> 00:05:50.992
Setup and orientation.

00:05:50.992 --> 00:05:54.895
Team data collection, two to
three weeks of fieldwork.

00:05:54.895 --> 00:05:57.931
Story collection, which runs alongside it.

00:05:57.931 --> 00:06:02.773
Analysis, where one click reads
both streams and drafts the report.

00:06:02.773 --> 00:06:03.785
And reporting.

00:06:03.785 --> 00:06:06.893
The phase marker is a
signpost, not a gate.

00:06:06.893 --> 00:06:09.423
It doesn't lock or unlock anything.

00:06:09.423 --> 00:06:14.555
A wave is one round of data collection:
findings, stories and a report.

00:06:14.555 --> 00:06:19.253
Starting a new wave keeps the
previous one and opens a fresh one.

00:06:19.253 --> 00:06:21.710
Programs can run up to four waves.

00:06:21.710 --> 00:06:25.758
Six to twelve months between
waves is the usual spacing,

00:06:25.758 --> 00:06:29.155
because sooner and you're
mostly reading noise.

00:06:29.155 --> 00:06:33.781
And keep the follow-up question
wording identical between waves,

00:06:33.781 --> 00:06:35.299
or you can't compare.

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:39.158
There are four roles, from
most to least access.

00:06:39.158 --> 00:06:44.544
The Program Lead has full control, and
is usually the client's project owner.

00:06:44.544 --> 00:06:46.642
Add a second lead as a backup.

00:06:46.642 --> 00:06:50.349
The Facilitator reads
everything and changes nothing,

00:06:50.349 --> 00:06:54.476
which is right for a Mosaic
consultant advising the client.

00:06:54.476 --> 00:06:58.533
A Team Member sees only their
own domain team's workspace,

00:06:58.533 --> 00:07:00.982
and each must be assigned a domain.

00:07:00.982 --> 00:07:04.129
And a Participant is
for record-keeping only,

00:07:04.129 --> 00:07:06.997
with no access to findings or the report.

00:07:06.997 --> 00:07:09.655
Most programs skip that role entirely.

00:07:09.655 --> 00:07:15.321
Invitees don't need an existing account,
and their membership shows only the dig,

00:07:15.321 --> 00:07:17.909
not the organization's other surveys.

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:20.110
Practise least privilege.

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:24.147
Drag each task to the
least-privileged role that can do it.

00:07:24.633 --> 00:07:28.278
Every new program shows a
Getting started checklist.

00:07:28.278 --> 00:07:30.450
First, have a license in place.

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:35.075
Then create the program, and the
seven teams appear automatically.

00:07:35.075 --> 00:07:38.720
Staff all seven teams, two
to four researchers each.

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:39.981
Add a second lead.

00:07:39.981 --> 00:07:44.606
Plan your dates: "Suggest dates"
sets findings due at three weeks,

00:07:44.606 --> 00:07:49.232
stories closing at five, analysis
at six, and the report at eight.

00:07:49.232 --> 00:07:55.048
Then set up story collection, which creates
and links the story survey with Culture

00:07:55.048 --> 00:07:57.081
Dig's own scorable questions.

00:07:57.081 --> 00:08:02.197
Start fieldwork, which moves the
program to phase two and can email every

00:08:02.197 --> 00:08:02.967
researcher.

00:08:02.967 --> 00:08:06.051
Approve at least one
prompt for each domain,

00:08:06.051 --> 00:08:10.536
because the public link stays
closed until a prompt is approved.

00:08:10.536 --> 00:08:14.531
Then copy the link and share
it by email, Slack or Teams.

00:08:14.531 --> 00:08:18.245
Teams get reminders before
and after their deadlines,

00:08:18.245 --> 00:08:21.188
and leads are told when a team is overdue.

00:08:21.700 --> 00:08:25.039
Each team records its
evidence as observations,

00:08:25.039 --> 00:08:27.809
and every observation has three fields.

00:08:27.809 --> 00:08:30.580
Source: role and context, never a name.

00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:34.913
For example, "night-shift charge
nurse, hospital, interview".

00:08:34.913 --> 00:08:38.679
Quote: what was said, as
near to verbatim as you can.

00:08:38.679 --> 00:08:43.794
And Theme: a short label, reusing the
same wording for the same pattern,

00:08:43.794 --> 00:08:47.132
because the analysis
looks for repeated themes.

00:08:47.132 --> 00:08:51.395
Make one entry per piece of
evidence, not one per interview.

00:08:51.395 --> 00:08:55.302
A forty-minute interview may
give you six observations.

00:08:55.302 --> 00:09:00.914
Never put a name in Source, not the
interviewee's, and not anyone they mention.

00:09:00.914 --> 00:09:03.472
When you interview, ask for a story,

00:09:03.472 --> 00:09:09.368
not an opinion: "Tell me about a time that
could only have happened here." And tell

00:09:09.368 --> 00:09:14.128
interviewees their words will be
attributed by role, never by name.

00:09:14.633 --> 00:09:20.252
Teams can also upload artifacts: anything
physical or digital that carries cultural

00:09:20.252 --> 00:09:25.329
meaning, like a photo of the break-room
notice board or an onboarding deck.

00:09:25.329 --> 00:09:28.240
Get permission before
photographing people.

00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:32.505
The team's synthesis box is its
section of the findings report,

00:09:32.505 --> 00:09:36.432
and whatever is in it at
submission flows into the report.

00:09:36.432 --> 00:09:41.577
Submitting locks the whole workspace,
and only a Program Lead can reopen it.

00:09:41.577 --> 00:09:44.961
So submit when the synthesis
is ready, not before.

00:09:45.467 --> 00:09:48.524
Here's an observation
a team member entered.

00:09:48.524 --> 00:09:50.539
It has more than one problem.

00:09:50.539 --> 00:09:52.624
Choose the most important fix.

00:09:53.133 --> 00:09:57.924
A story collection asks a pool of
prompts, at least one per domain.

00:09:57.924 --> 00:10:01.929
Each respondent is served one
approved prompt at random,

00:10:01.929 --> 00:10:04.574
and offered another when they finish.

00:10:04.574 --> 00:10:09.723
Prompts can be AI drafts, which a
person always reviews before approval,

00:10:09.723 --> 00:10:14.299
hand-written, or chosen from a
library of thirty-two per domain.

00:10:14.299 --> 00:10:19.448
If a Culture Mosaic is linked, its
three lowest subcomponents are named,

00:10:19.448 --> 00:10:21.736
and prompts on those come first.

00:10:21.736 --> 00:10:24.167
Respondents don't need an account.

00:10:24.167 --> 00:10:29.244
The page promises one story and a
few questions in about three minutes,

00:10:29.244 --> 00:10:32.820
says that stories are read
by AI to find patterns,

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:37.182
and asks people to write about
what happened rather than who.

00:10:37.182 --> 00:10:41.043
Afterwards, they get a link
to delete their own story.

00:10:41.043 --> 00:10:43.331
And know the three stop buttons.

00:10:43.331 --> 00:10:47.550
Close collection stops new
stories, and you can reverse it.

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:50.625
Unlink breaks the link
to the story survey.

00:10:50.625 --> 00:10:53.628
And Mark program complete closes the link,

00:10:53.628 --> 00:10:56.846
and reopening the program
does not reopen it.

00:10:57.333 --> 00:11:01.533
Culture Dig uses the same
question types as Mosaic Signal,

00:11:01.533 --> 00:11:07.252
with one big difference: its questions
have a direction, so they can be scored.

00:11:07.252 --> 00:11:11.741
The triad asks what was really
driving people: shared purpose,

00:11:11.741 --> 00:11:14.637
getting the job done, or avoiding blame.

00:11:14.637 --> 00:11:17.533
Avoiding blame is the concerning corner.

00:11:17.533 --> 00:11:23.542
One slider asks whether it felt made up as
we went, or like we knew what to expect.

00:11:23.542 --> 00:11:28.755
Another asks whether the story left
people less or more connected to the

00:11:28.755 --> 00:11:29.696
organization.

00:11:29.696 --> 00:11:34.258
The stones sit on two fixed axes:
unpredictable to predictable,

00:11:34.258 --> 00:11:36.212
and draining to energising.

00:11:36.212 --> 00:11:38.095
That makes four quadrants.

00:11:38.095 --> 00:11:40.846
Predictable and energising is Healthy.

00:11:40.846 --> 00:11:44.032
Unpredictable and
energising is Complicated.

00:11:44.032 --> 00:11:46.493
Predictable and draining is Rigid.

00:11:46.493 --> 00:11:49.534
And unpredictable and draining is Chaotic.

00:11:49.534 --> 00:11:54.457
Standalone Mosaic Signal questions
are written for open exploration,

00:11:54.457 --> 00:11:58.222
which is right for sense-making
but can't be scored.

00:11:58.222 --> 00:12:03.580
Culture Dig fixes the direction so it
can compute a score for each domain.

00:12:04.067 --> 00:12:09.585
Here's how one domain gets its number,
a composite from zero to one hundred.

00:12:09.585 --> 00:12:15.393
Take the sliders: on average, how far
toward the healthier end did stories land?

00:12:15.393 --> 00:12:16.700
Say sixty percent.

00:12:16.700 --> 00:12:20.621
Take the stones: of all the
placements in this domain,

00:12:20.621 --> 00:12:23.525
what share landed in the Healthy corner?

00:12:23.525 --> 00:12:24.614
Say forty-five.

00:12:24.614 --> 00:12:29.551
Take the triad: how much of the
weight sat away from avoiding blame?

00:12:29.551 --> 00:12:30.422
Say seventy.

00:12:30.422 --> 00:12:35.650
Weight them thirty-five percent,
thirty-five percent and thirty percent,

00:12:35.650 --> 00:12:38.627
and add them up: fifty-seven point eight.

00:12:38.627 --> 00:12:44.508
That formula is locked by the Mosaic team,
and scores are computed by the server,

00:12:44.508 --> 00:12:46.105
never entered by hand.

00:12:46.105 --> 00:12:47.920
There are two safeguards.

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:53.220
No domain gets a score until its
thinnest input has at least ten stories.

00:12:53.220 --> 00:12:56.197
Until then you'll see "too few to score".

00:12:56.197 --> 00:13:00.699
And every score carries a
ninety-five percent margin of error,

00:13:00.699 --> 00:13:03.385
so fewer stories means a wider range.

00:13:03.385 --> 00:13:08.250
Before you compare two domains,
check whether their ranges overlap.

00:13:08.733 --> 00:13:13.611
A high composite isn't a good culture,
and a low one isn't a bad culture.

00:13:13.611 --> 00:13:18.824
It tells you how the stories in a domain
read: how predictable and energising,

00:13:18.824 --> 00:13:23.902
how far toward the healthier end, and
how little blame-avoidance drove them.

00:13:23.902 --> 00:13:25.773
It's a pattern, not a grade.

00:13:25.773 --> 00:13:30.585
If two domains' ranges overlap, don't
claim one is lower than the other.

00:13:30.585 --> 00:13:32.656
And look underneath the number.

00:13:32.656 --> 00:13:36.532
A low composite could come from
stones landing in Chaotic,

00:13:36.532 --> 00:13:41.143
from stones landing in Rigid, or
from heavy weight on avoiding blame,

00:13:41.143 --> 00:13:44.417
and each points to a very
different conversation.

00:13:44.417 --> 00:13:49.830
The Mosaic team's own testing on a simulated
wave found the ranking stayed stable

00:13:49.830 --> 00:13:55.175
under different weightings, and that in
practice the composite moves mostly with

00:13:55.175 --> 00:13:55.910
the stones.

00:13:56.400 --> 00:13:58.968
Here's how the report comes together.

00:13:58.968 --> 00:14:03.617
On Story patterns, fill from stories,
review the figures, and save,

00:14:03.617 --> 00:14:06.324
which computes each domain's composite.

00:14:06.324 --> 00:14:11.737
You can also link a Culture Mosaic there
to see which of its items each domain

00:14:11.737 --> 00:14:13.402
confirms or contradicts.

00:14:13.402 --> 00:14:17.289
Approve the AI summaries of
what the stories were about.

00:14:17.289 --> 00:14:21.036
Then press Analyze, which
drafts an executive summary,

00:14:21.036 --> 00:14:24.992
plus findings and two to four
recommendations per domain,

00:14:24.992 --> 00:14:29.364
and treats disagreement between
teams and stories as a finding.

00:14:29.364 --> 00:14:32.903
Then edit every section
yourself, saving as you go.

00:14:32.903 --> 00:14:36.165
Approve up to three
verbatim quotes per domain,

00:14:36.165 --> 00:14:39.357
and nothing prints until
a person approves it.

00:14:39.357 --> 00:14:44.354
There are no quotes from a domain with
fewer than ten stories that wave.

00:14:44.354 --> 00:14:49.142
Move the report from Draft, to In
review, to Final, which freezes it.

00:14:49.142 --> 00:14:51.294
Then print or save it as a PDF.

00:14:51.294 --> 00:14:57.054
The report includes a "Where to start" page
that groups domains rather than ranking

00:14:57.054 --> 00:15:01.912
them, a score table with story counts
and ranges, one page per domain,

00:15:01.912 --> 00:15:03.300
and the methodology.

00:15:03.800 --> 00:15:09.099
Culture changes slowly, and a second
wave tells you whether it's moving.

00:15:09.099 --> 00:15:11.969
Space waves six to twelve months apart.

00:15:11.969 --> 00:15:17.636
Keep the follow-up question wording
identical, and reuse last wave's prompts.

00:15:17.636 --> 00:15:23.524
Read change with the ranges: if the ranges
overlap a lot, the change isn't firm.

00:15:23.524 --> 00:15:29.486
Story summaries only flag a theme as
growing or shrinking when a statistical test

00:15:29.486 --> 00:15:30.001
passes.

00:15:30.500 --> 00:15:31.545
Now plan one.

00:15:31.545 --> 00:15:33.073
It's November 2026.

00:15:33.073 --> 00:15:38.541
Northwind's Collaboration is improving,
but Empowered Teams is flat,

00:15:38.541 --> 00:15:44.250
and "decisions made without frontline
input" is a steady comment theme.

00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:48.351
Alex wants to understand
why decisions feel closed,

00:15:48.351 --> 00:15:54.703
and is considering a Culture Dig that
Northwind runs itself, with you advising.

00:15:54.703 --> 00:16:00.573
Draft a one-page plan covering the
license, roles, teams, dates, prompts,

00:16:00.573 --> 00:16:02.583
communications and waves.

00:16:02.583 --> 00:16:05.478
Then compare it with the model plan.

00:16:05.967 --> 00:16:11.158
Time for the module check: ten
questions, and you need eight right.

00:16:11.158 --> 00:16:12.863
Retakes are unlimited.

00:16:13.367 --> 00:16:18.277
Here's your Culture Dig Program
Planner, with roles, teams, dates,

00:16:18.277 --> 00:16:20.807
story collection and report steps.

00:16:20.807 --> 00:16:21.700
Download it.

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:27.650
And here's the Field Card for your researchers:
the three parts of an observation,

00:16:27.650 --> 00:16:32.701
good interview questions, and what to
do before, during and after fieldwork.

00:16:33.200 --> 00:16:33.901
To recap.

00:16:33.901 --> 00:16:40.212
Culture Dig is an eight to twelve week
research program on Schein's three layers,

00:16:40.212 --> 00:16:44.186
with seven domains, seven
teams and one story link.

00:16:44.186 --> 00:16:50.497
Teams bring depth, stories bring breadth,
and the gaps between them are findings.

00:16:50.497 --> 00:16:56.575
Roles run from Program Lead, to Facilitator,
who is read-only, to Team Member,

00:16:56.575 --> 00:16:57.743
to Participant.

00:16:57.743 --> 00:17:03.353
Observations are source, quote and
theme, and source never holds a name.

00:17:03.353 --> 00:17:08.574
The composite needs ten or more
stories and always carries a range.

00:17:08.574 --> 00:17:10.755
It's a pattern, not a grade.

00:17:10.755 --> 00:17:15.041
And a website purchase buys
software, not facilitation.

00:17:15.041 --> 00:17:18.235
Next: Module 8, interpreting Mosaic data.

